<DetailLayout title="15 Mistakes to Avoid in Guilin & Yangshuo" subtitle="Don't learn the hard way. Common mistakes and how to avoid them."
  description="15 common mistakes travelers make in Guilin and Yangshuo — and how to avoid them. Scam awareness, timing, and practical tips.">
  <h2>{"#"}1: Booking the Wrong Li River Section</h2>
  <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Paying for a "Li River cruise" that takes you on a short, less scenic section or a different river entirely. <strong>How to avoid:</strong> Book the Yangdi-to-Xingping section — this is the iconic stretch. Confirm the exact piers when booking.</p>

  <h2>{"#"}2: Not Bringing Cash</h2>
  <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Assuming everywhere takes cards or mobile payments. Many bamboo raft operators, countryside restaurants, and small guesthouses are cash-only. <strong>How to avoid:</strong> Carry at least 500 RMB in cash before leaving Guilin city.</p>

  <h2>{"#"}3: Visiting During Golden Week (Oct 1-7)</h2>
  <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Arriving during China's biggest national holiday. Hotels triple in price, attractions are shoulder-to-shoulder, and Li River cruise tickets sell out days ahead. <strong>How to avoid:</strong> Check the Chinese holiday calendar before booking. Golden Week and Spring Festival are the worst times.</p>

  <h2>{"#"}4: Trusting Touts at Bus and Train Stations</h2>
  <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Following someone who approaches you at the station offering a "cheap ride" or "special tour." These are almost always scams. <strong>How to avoid:</strong> Use official taxi queues, the Didi app, or pre-arranged transfers. Ignore everyone who approaches you unsolicited.</p>

  <h2>{"#"}5: Overpaying for Tours Booked Online</h2>
  <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Booking Li River cruises and tours through international platforms that add 50-100% markup. <strong>How to avoid:</strong> Book through your hotel (they charge a small commission but handle logistics) or directly at the official pier ticket office.</p>

  <h2>{"#"}6: Not Checking the Weather</h2>
  <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Arriving unprepared for rain. Guilin gets significant rainfall year-round. <strong>How to avoid:</strong> Pack a rain jacket and waterproof shoes. Check the forecast before booking outdoor activities. Misty days can still be beautiful for photos.</p>

  <h2>{"#"}7: Staying Too Far from Yangshuo</h2>
  <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Booking a hotel marketed as "Yangshuo" that is actually 15-20 km away. <strong>How to avoid:</strong> Check the hotel location on a map. Ideally within 5 km of Yangshuo town center.</p>

  <h2>{"#"}8: Skipping the Countryside Bike Ride</h2>
  <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Only seeing the Li River and West Street, missing Yangshuo's best experience. <strong>How to avoid:</strong> Rent a bike or e-bike for at least one day. The Ten Mile Gallery road and the Yulong River paths are unforgettable.</p>

  <h2>{"#"}9: Not Downloading Offline Maps</h2>
  <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Assuming Google Maps works reliably in rural China. It doesn't. <strong>How to avoid:</strong> Download offline maps before you leave your hotel. Baidu Maps is more accurate for rural roads but requires some Chinese reading ability.</p>

  <h2>{"#"}10: Wearing the Wrong Shoes</h2>
  <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Hiking Moon Hill in flip-flops. Or wearing new white sneakers in the rain. <strong>How to avoid:</strong> Pack sturdy walking shoes with good grip. The stone steps at Moon Hill and the paths in Longji get slippery when wet.</p>

  <h2>{"#"}11-15: Quick Hits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>{"#"}11:</strong> Not learning "bu yao" (I don't want it) — the most useful phrase for declining persistent vendors.</li>
    <li><strong>{"#"}12:</strong> Ordering the tourist-priced menu. Look for restaurants where locals eat — the menu will be in Chinese but prices will be 30-50% lower.</li>
    <li><strong>{"#"}13:</strong> Not carrying toilet paper. Public toilets in rural areas often don't provide it.</li>
    <li><strong>{"#"}14:</strong> Assuming you can use your credit card for everything. Even with Alipay set up, some rural vendors are cash-only.</li>
    <li><strong>{"#"}15:</strong> Rushing. Yangshuo rewards slow travel. A rushed 1-day visit will feel like a tourist conveyor belt. Three days minimum.</li>
  </ul>

